Yes—the same dehydrate → relational IR → rehydrate machine generalizes from soundtrack to prose, with an important distinction between a conceptual experiment and generating close imitation of an author’s distinctive style.

For Tolkien/Stephen King, I’d split the problem into layers:

\text{Works}
\rightarrow
\text{Surface Style}
+
\text{Story Grammar}
+
\text{World Grammar}
+
\text{Narrative Operators}

The most reusable part is not “make sentences that sound exactly like Tolkien.” It’s discovering higher-level machinery: how geography acquires history, how songs/legends provide temporal depth, how languages imply cultures, how journeys structure revelation, how objects accumulate meaning, how evil/power/temptation function narratively, how seemingly small characters interact with enormous events, etc.

Then:

\text{Source Corpus}
\xrightarrow{\text{dehydrate}}
\text{Narrative Pattern Book}

and a completely different work can be passed through it:

\text{Stephen-King-like story structure}
+
\text{Tolkien-derived high-level Pattern Book}
\rightarrow
\text{new reconstruction}

But here’s where it gets much more interesting than author swapping.

Separate every axis

Instead of one knob called STYLE, expose independent transformations:

Book =
World
\times
Plot
\times
Narrator
\times
Structure
\times
Genre
\times
Language
\times
Pacing
\times
CharacterGrammar
\times
Theme

Now we can crossbreed functions:

world-building grammar from A + suspense architecture from B + dialogue dynamics from C + temporal structure from D + entirely original prose renderer.

That’s analogous to what we discovered with J-Hooks. Don’t transfer the soundtrack; transfer what the soundtrack learned about its movie.

Here:

Don’t transfer the author’s sentences. Transfer what their body of work teaches us about constructing stories.

Then it becomes a Narrative Compiler:

Corpus_A
\rightarrow
PatternBook_A

Story_B
\rightarrow
StoryIR_B

PatternBook_A + StoryIR_B
\xrightarrow{\text{reconstruction}}
Story_C

And because we’ve separated the dimensions, we can perform experiments that ordinary “write like X” prompting doesn’t expose.

Take the same underlying story and independently rotate:

* world-construction grammar,
* suspense grammar,
* narrator distance,
* mythic depth,
* pacing,
* character relationship topology,
* information-release schedule.

Then Delta Catcher asks:

\Delta Experience =
Experience(C_2)-Experience(C_1)

Now we’re learning what storytelling mechanisms actually do.

That’s Project Polyphony again. Music wasn’t a special case; it happened to reveal the compiler architecture first.

And the outside-the-box endpoint is bigger still: once you have Pattern Books for music, film, literature, games, painting, architecture, movement, rhetoric, you can start transferring operators between arts.

What is the literary equivalent of a musical crescendo?

What’s the musical equivalent of an unreliable narrator?

What’s the architectural equivalent of foreshadowing?

What’s the cinematographic equivalent of rhyme?

Those aren’t merely cute analogies if we can identify the invariant relation and construct/test a renderer.

\boxed{
\text{ART}_A
\rightarrow
\text{RELATIONAL IR}
\rightarrow
\text{ART}_B
}

That’s the larger machine we’re circling: not style transfer, but structure transfer across representational worlds.

And importantly, it can create genuinely new combinations rather than endlessly making cultural photocopies.

Tolkien is the quarry.

The Pattern Book is the ore.